{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And they shall place My name upon the children of Israel.</b> When they mention My name upon them, I shall bless them all — priests, Levites, and Israelites — for the Name appears in each and every verse of the three. And further, by way of the Midrash: in these three verses, the first verse has three words, the second has five, and the third has seven, and the mnemonic is גה״ז (3, 5, 7). The first verse corresponds to the three patriarchs, that He remember the covenant of the fathers; the second verse corresponds to the five books of the Torah, for the Torah was given in the merit of the fathers; the third verse corresponds to the seven firmaments. I found in Yerushalmi of Sanhedrin: for the intercalation of the year they begin with three, and they deliberate with five, and they conclude with seven. With three corresponding to 'יברכך,' with five corresponding to 'יאר,' with seven corresponding to 'ישא'; with three corresponding to the keepers of the gate, with five corresponding to the five who see the face of the king, with seven corresponding to the seven who see the face of the king." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: יברכך ה׳ וישמרך — the order of these blessings is from below upward, in the same order as the intention in the offerings. For the priest would bless Israel with these blessings after he had completed the act of the offering, as it is written (Leviticus 9:22): ״ויברכם וירד מעשות החטאת והעולה והשלמים,״ and its meaning is 'and he blessed them' after he had descended, and at the time he was offering the offering he would first direct his intention toward the ה (heh), which is the final ה of the Name, as I explained in the section ויקרא regarding the addition of the ה of המזבחה; and from the ה to the וא (vav), and from there onward to the pure thought, for the essence of the intention in the offering is to draw down will from the pure thought to His supernal Names, for thereby the supernal dwelling and the lower dwelling are blessed. And this is the secret of these three verses in this order: יברכך ה׳ וישמרך — this is the attribute of שמור, and this is the language of וישמרך; יאר ה׳ is the middle line, and פניו are its own countenance, which are the countenance of mercy; ישא ה׳ is Binah, and the word פניו is the attribute of fear, which He removes from upon you, and then upon its removal He establishes for you peace — and these are the three patriarchs. For the attribute of mercy is rolled upward into חסד alone, and then there is peace without fear. And this matter is explained from below upward, and the language of blessing in each and every verse increases and ascends. And understand this, for these three blessings encompass all ten Sefirot — thus I have received." "<b>But</b> the opinion of the Ramban in his commentary is that these blessings are from above downward, and their explanation is thus: יברכך ה׳ — the intention is that He bestows His blessing by way of the middle line as far as שמור; this is 'וישמרך.' He guards for their attribute, which is Knesset Israel, and the guarding depends upon the hand of Israel, and thereby they are guarded. יאר — the middle line; פניו — that is, the light of His Shekhinah for your need; ויחנך — that He provide favor for your attribute, for through this the blessing and the favor come to Israel. ישא ה׳ פניו — that is, that He lift it to Himself, in the manner of what is said (Deuteronomy 32:40): ״כי אשא אל שמים ידי״ — to Himself, for your need; וישם לך — for your need — peace between Him and your attribute, for peace is what decides between mercy and judgment. 'ושמו את שמי' — the unified Name; 'ואני' — in the language of (Exodus 15:26): ״כי אני ה׳ רופאך.״ And behold, this is clear. And I have already mentioned above in the verse 'אמור להם' that there were times when they would mention the Explicit Name in public, and this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted: 'כה תברכו — בשם המפורש.' And these three Names are called the Explicit Name of twelve, and thus the priest would pronounce them according to the Sefer ha-Bahir: יברכך in the יפעל form, יאר in יפעל, ישא in יפעל, and thus it is interpreted as a Divine Name on the pattern of 'ה׳ מלך ה׳ מלך ה׳ ימלוך.' And if you are perceptive enough to vocalize the three יודי״ן of יברכך, יאר, ישא, then you will understand 'the fear of YHWH' and find 'the knowledge of the holy ones' — for the vowel-points in their pointing allude to the mention of the Explicit Name in the matter. And understand this." "<b>However,</b> the tradition of Ashkenaz I received in a whispered manner: הדרה הרודף ירדוף, and the discerning one will understand. And I have already written on the verse (Exodus 17:12) ״ויהי ידיו אמונה״ that the priest, when lifting his hands, would form the letters of the Name הוי״ה with his hands. But there are those who explained that he would form the letters of the Name שדי: ד״ל with the thumb, יו״ד with the little finger, which is the smallest, שי״ן with the three middle fingers." "<b>And you will find</b> further in Sefer ha-Bahir: יברכך ה׳, יאר ה׳, ישא ה׳ — this is the Name of twelve letters, teaching that the names of the Holy One, blessed be He, are three hosts (חילים), and each host resembles its fellow, and its name is like its name, and all are sealed with יו״ד ה״א וא״ו ה״א. Twenty-four times יו״ד ה״א וא״ו ה״א — that is one host; and so the second, and so the third; and that is seventy-two names of the Holy One, blessed be He. Come and learn: Gevurah is the prince over all the holy forms from the left side of the Holy One, blessed be He, and this is Gabriel; and from His right, a prince over all the holy forms, and this is Michael; and in the middle is the Truth, who is prince over all the holy forms; and each prince has twenty-four forms, and there is no number to His troops, as it is said (Job 25:3): ״היש מספר לגדודיו.״ If so, they are seventy-two and seventy-two. He said to him: No — for when Israel offer an offering before their Father in heaven, they are unified together, and that is the unification of our Elohim. And why is it called קרבן (offering)? Because on account of its bringing near (מקרב) the holy forms, as we say (Ezekiel 37:17): ״וקרב אותם אחד אל אחד לך לעץ אחד והיו לאחדים בידך.״ And we say: ריח ניחוח — and 'ריח' is only in the nose, and 'נשימה,' which is the spirit, is only in the nose, and 'ניחוח' is nothing but descent, as it is written (Leviticus 9): ״וירד,״ which is translated ונחת. The spirit descends and is unified with the holy forms and is brought near by way of the offering — and that is why it is called קרבן. Until here. After you understand this, it will be explained to you what the intention of the princes was in the dedication of the altar, that they offered twenty-four bulls, as it is written (Numbers 7:88): ״וכל בקר זבח השלמים עשרים וארבעה פרים,״ for the intention of the offering was to supply each and every attribute from Him; therefore they offered twenty-four bulls, corresponding to the one host, which is from the left side, namely the attribute of Gevurah, and they are twenty-four names, as was explained in the Midrash ha-Bahir." "<b>And you must</b> know that in the first verse there are 15 (י״ה) letters, and in the second 15 (י״ה) letters, and in the third 15 (יה״י) letters — and this indicates that the great Name mentioned in them three times rules over three times: the present, the past, and the future. And so you have sixty letters, and concerning them Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Song of Songs 3:7): ״ששים גבורים סביב לה מגבורי ישראל״ — 'sixty mighty men surround it, of the mighty men of Israel.'" "<b>And the reason</b> he began the allusion with the present is that all times are in the present with respect to the Holy One, blessed be He, for nothing of His years has passed — in the manner of what is written (Psalms 102:28): ״ואתה הוא ושנותיך לא יתמו״ — 'and You are He, and Your years shall not end.' And therefore we join together in our prayer and say: 'YHWH reigned, YHWH reigns, YHWH shall reign forever and ever,' to allude to the three parts of time, which are the present, the past, and the future. Now you already know that ״ה׳ מלך ה׳ מלך ה׳ ימלוך לעולם ועד״ is not a single verse but rather scattered verses that you will find in the book of Psalms in this order. And he mentioned first the matter of the present — this you will find in the tenth psalm, which is למה ה׳ תעמוד ברחוק (Psalms 10), and then ״ה׳ מלך עולם ועד אבדו גוים מארצו״ (Psalms 10:16). And observe that David did not mention the kingship of heaven until this psalm, which is the tenth. And you will also find in the twentieth psalm — which is the tenth from the tenth — that he mentioned in it ״ה׳ הושיעה המלך יעננו ביום קראנו״ (Psalms 20:10). And afterward he mentioned throughout the book the language of king in the past tense, as he said (Psalms 47): ״מלך אלהים על גוים״, and further said (Psalms 96): ״אמרו בגוים ה׳ מלך״, and further said (Psalms 97): ״ה׳ מלך תגל הארץ״, and further (Psalms 99): ״ה׳ מלך ירגזו עמים״. And afterward, at the end of the book, he mentioned the future and said (Psalms 146): ״ימלוך ה׳ לעולם אלהיך ציון לדור ודור הללויה״. And so you have it that David mentioned all three in order. And it is known that ״ה׳ ימלוך לעולם ועד״ is a complete verse — Moses said it in the Song at the Sea." "<b>Know that</b> from the verse of יברכך there emerges a Name of 22 from יברכך through יאר ה׳ — you will find 22 letters — and it emerges from it by substitution of אלפ״א בית״א (alphabetical atbash), and prayer in a time of distress is heard through it, and it is traced from the sixth, which is the attribute of Jacob."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:41.759012681Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}